</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cervical cord</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">24 (100%)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">30 (93.8%)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.21</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">54 (96.4%)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Thoracic cord</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 (8.3%)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 (18.8%)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.27</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8 (14.3%)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Infratentorial lesions</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12 (50.0%)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20 (62.5%)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.35</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">32 (57.1%)</td></tr></tbody></table></alternatives><table-wrap-foot><fn id="t001fn001"><p>EDSS: Expanded Disability Status Scale; PPMS: Primary Progressive Multiple Sclerosis; RRMS: Relapsing-Remitting Multiple Sclerosis; SPMS: Secondary Progressive Multiple Sclerosis.</p></fn><fn id="t001fn002"><p>*Categorical variables were compared using the Chi-square test, and means were compared using the Mann-Whitney U test.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>Across the MS cohort, OND, ONSD, and the OND/ONSD ratio were not significantly associated with sex, age, MS type, EDSS score, disease duration, family history of MS, DMDs, number of lesions, or cervical/thoracic lesions. Infratentorial lesions were associated with smaller right ONSD (p&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.017). A history of right-sided ON was associated with smaller right OND and ONSD (both p&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.001), and a history of left-sided ON was associated with smaller left OND (p&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.001) and ONSD (p&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.002) (<xref rid="pone.0336564.t002" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref>).</p><table-wrap position="float" id="pone.0336564.t002"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0336564.t002</object-id><label>Table 2</label><caption><title>Relationships between OND, ONSD, and OND/ONSD ratio, and demographic, clinical and paraclinical characteristics of patients with MS (p-value).</title></caption><alternatives><graphic xlink:href="pone.0336564.t002" id="pone.0336564.t002g" position="float"/><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" valign="middle" span="1"/><col align="left" valign="middle" span="1"/><col align="left" valign="middle" span="1"/><col align="left" valign="middle" span="1"/><col align="left" valign="middle" span="1"/><col align="left" valign="middle" span="1"/><col align="left" valign="middle" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Rt OND</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Lt OND</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Rt ONSD</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Lt ONSD</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Rt OND/ONSD</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Lt OND/ONSD</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" colspan="7" rowspan="1">
